{"user":"O_oNevermore","message":"I read a short history book that went through... history ig and kept showing the same pattern over and over again\n\nthe large fat lazy organized and safe settlers exist in a fixed region with their institutions that provide for them\n\nand the nomads roam and sustain themselves in harsh conditions\n\nand over and over again the nomads come down from their hills or their wilderness and invade and conquer the settlers and become their rulers\n\neither destroying their institutions or assimilating completely\n\nit's a lot like how DNA is said to be the invasion of a virus into the host of a bacteria which eventually merge into one permanently and the virus becomes the mitochondria\n\nthe moral of the story is, it doesn't matter if you think you are right, if your ideas are scalable, if your society is stable or if your enemies are immoral\n\nthe strong rule over the weak","timestamp":"2026-09-08T11:11:41.582000+00:00","channel":"☯️fnord-log","channel_id":"discord:1474441056383799348","thread":null,"thread_id":null,"reply_to":null,"id":"discord:1546840447467520061","user_id":"discord:335107602243518466","is_bot":false,"url":"https://discord.com/channels/1344300408893345863/1474441056383799348/1546840447467520061","attachments":[],"platform":"discord","server":"ORI (est. 2025)","server_id":"discord:1344300408893345863"}
{"user":"O_oNevermore","message":"jorden Peterson often calls the Bible the \"biblical library\" on stage when giving lectures \n\nthis got me thinking about words (again). this is something I can use as an example to illustrate this simple word trick (which acts as a constructor that has measurable phenomnonological effects)\n\ncalling the Bible a \"biblical library\" does 2 things. firstly, it expands the ontological detail. you might not know the Bible is not one book with chapters and that it is rather a collection of books. this detail helps explain things like the inconsistency in style or content across chapters (which you might have implicitly wondered about, as a vague proxy question of \"why does this text have an unfamiliar type of complexity\"). this is something almost everyone already knows at this point, so I'd posit the main purpose of this word choice is something else.\n\nthat brings us to the second thing it does. it changes the axiological weighting, calling it the Bible or a book is a very compressed concept, it streamlines the understanding, throwing away a lot of depth in favor of efficiency. calling it the \"biblical library\" breaks out of the mold and adds more depth back to it, it's an unfamiliar and more particular way of framing a familiar concept, it adds a richness to the connotation landscape or semantic network, which might inadvertently increase the value placed on the book in your mind through framing\n\nthese axiological movements are just as important in rhetoric and communication as ontological extrapolation\n\nit would in fact be impossible to understand what is being said if we only account for ontology and not axiology (which is the exact problem with legalese and classical text)\n\nhow information is weighted in value space is how it gets packed into the mind of the audience\n\nhow information should be packed (the aesthetic) is as an objective question as any ontological question, even if our methods to answer this are more rudimentary (consisting almost entirely of feeling)","timestamp":"2026-09-08T11:47:40.234000+00:00","channel":"✍️eval-log","channel_id":"discord:1409673828673523762","thread":null,"thread_id":null,"reply_to":null,"id":"discord:1546849501510246450","user_id":"discord:335107602243518466","is_bot":false,"url":"https://discord.com/channels/1344300408893345863/1409673828673523762/1546849501510246450","attachments":[],"platform":"discord","server":"ORI (est. 2025)","server_id":"discord:1344300408893345863"}
